{"slug":"/students/subjects/physics/fluids/viscosity/","source":"students/subjects/physics/fluids/viscosity.html","area":"students","kind":"content","title":"Viscosity & Flow | Physics","description":"","subject":"physics","body":"<div class=\"max-w-4xl mx-auto\">\n    <nav class=\"mb-6\"><a href=\"/students/subjects/physics/fluids/\" class=\"text-blue-600\">← Back to Fluids</a></nav>\n    <article class=\"content-card rounded-xl p-8\">\n      <h1 class=\"text-2xl font-bold mb-2\">Viscosity &amp; Flow</h1>\n      <p class=\"text-gray-700 mb-4\">Viscosity measures a fluid's resistance to flow. Stoke's law for small spheres: F = 6π η r v.</p>\n\n      <section class=\"mb-4\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold\">Key formula</h2>\n        <p class=\"bg-gray-50 p-3 rounded\">Stoke's drag: F = 6π η r v (η is dynamic viscosity)</p>\n      </section>\n\n      <section class=\"mb-4\">\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold\">Practice</h2>\n        <ol class=\"list-decimal list-inside\">\n          <li>Describe how viscosity affects terminal velocity of a falling sphere.</li>\n          <li>Using Stoke's law, compute drag on a 1 mm radius sphere moving at 0.1 m/s in a fluid with η = 0.001 Pa·s.</li>\n        </ol>\n      </section>\n\n      <section>\n        <h2 class=\"font-semibold\">Solutions (brief)</h2>\n        <ol class=\"list-decimal list-inside\">\n          <li>Higher viscosity increases drag, reducing terminal velocity.</li>\n          <li>F = 6π×0.001×0.001×0.1 ≈ 1.88×10⁻⁶ N.</li>\n        </ol>\n      </section>\n    </article>\n  </div>\n<!-- Topic Quiz System -->","bodyClass":"min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-indigo-100 p-6","bodyStyle":"","css":["body{font-family:Inter, sans-serif} .content-card{-webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(16px);backdrop-filter:blur(16px);background:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.2);box-shadow:0 8px 32px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);}"],"quiz":null,"tracker":null,"links":[]}